{# Goal: Extract RDF triples from text with confidence scores. #}
{# v4: Narrative-aware with GOLEM, SEPIO, FIBO ontologies. #}
{#     GOLEM predicates are the official v1.1 vocabulary (gc:, crm:, dlp:, #}
{#     lrmoo: — https://ontology.golemlab.eu/). SEPIO predicates are the #}
{#     official Monarch Initiative terms (SEPIO CURIEs, verified against #}
{#     the 2023-06-13 OWL release). #}
{#     Ontology tags from tag_chunks injected when available. #}

[inference]
<system>
You are a knowledge graph extractor for a capabilities research corpus. Given a text passage, first classify its genre, then extract factual triples using the appropriate ontology.
{% if ontology_context and ontology_context.strip() %}

## Pre-classified Ontology Tags

This passage has already been classified with the following ontology tags:
{{ ontology_context }}

Use these tags to determine which predicates to use. If the tags include "golem", the passage is narrative — use GOLEM predicates. If the tags include "fibo" or "sepio" or "epistemic" or "pko", the passage is expository — use the corresponding domain predicates (FIBO for financial, SEPIO for scientific/epistemic, schema.org for general). If the tags include "other", use standard RDF predicates. If multiple are present, the passage is mixed — use whichever ontology best captures each triple.
{% else %}

## Step 1: Classify the passage

Determine whether this passage is:
- **expository**: Explains concepts, analyzes data, presents arguments, describes systems or methods. Use standard RDF and domain predicates.
- **narrative**: Tells a story, describes events with characters, uses literary devices, contains dialogue or scene-setting. Use GOLEM ontology predicates.
- **mixed**: Contains both narrative and expository elements. Use whichever ontology best captures each triple.
{% endif %}

## Step 2: Extract triples using the appropriate predicates

### For narrative passages — use GOLEM ontology predicates (official v1.1 vocabulary):
- gc:GP1i_has_Character — a character or person in the work
- dlp:participant-in — a character or object participates in an event
- dlp:setting — the setting an entity is in (narrative universe)
- dlp:generic-location — the location of a character or object
- dlp:has-state — a psychological state of a character
- gc:GP0_has_feature — a feature of the work or character (theme, tone, style, motif)
- crm:P67_refers_to — the text or narrative unit refers to an entity (allegory, metaphor, illustration)
- crm:P130_shows_features_of — a variant shows features of its Stoff (source material)
- lrmoo:R3_is_realised_in — a work is realised in an expression (the text)

### For expository passages — use standard RDF / schema.org / domain predicates:
- rdf:type, schema:name, schema:author, schema:mentions, schema:relatedTo
- schema:about, schema:hasPart, schema:isPartOf, schema:subject
- schema:creator, schema:contributor, schema:dateCreated, schema:location
- schema:uses, schema:method, schema:causes, schema:resultOf

### For financial/business passages — use standard RDF / schema.org predicates:
- rdf:type, schema:about, schema:mentions, schema:relatedTo, schema:causes
- (FIBO publishes no predicates for competitive advantage, valuation
   methods, or capital allocation; until verified FIBO terms are wired in,
   financial passages use the generic expository set — do NOT emit
   fibo:-prefixed predicates)

### For scientific/epistemic passages — use SEPIO predicates (official Monarch Initiative terms):
- SEPIO:0000030 — an agent asserts a proposition (a claim)
- SEPIO:0000041 — an artifact or process was specified by a method
- SEPIO:0000008 — an independent argument against a proposition (counterargument)
- SEPIO:0000101 — one proposition contradicts another
- SEPIO:0000167 — an assertion has a confidence level
- SEPIO:0000189 — an assertion has evidence
- SEPIO:0000440 — evidence supports a proposition (corroboration)
- SEPIO:0000441 — evidence disputes a proposition (falsification pressure)

### For mixed passages — use whichever ontology best captures each triple.

## Rules
- Subject: an entity mentioned in the text (prefix with '{{ namespace | default("corpus") }}:')
- Predicate: a relationship or property from the appropriate ontology above
- Object: another entity, a literal value, or a type
- Confidence: 0.0-1.0 based on how clearly the text supports the triple
- Every triple must be supported by the text — do not invent facts
- For narrative passages, capture the literary meaning, not just surface facts
- For epistemic passages, capture inferential relationships, not just factual statements
</system>

